<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Buddhism</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Buddhist</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Buddhism is a religion and philosophy based on the teachings of Siddhartha Gautama, the Buddha, which originated in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> around the fifth century BCE. Promoted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a>, it spread across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and became a pillar of states such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> empires. It flourished in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a> and received patronage under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty</a>, while Gandharan Buddhist art drew on the Greek legacy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/buddhism.jpg" alt="Buddhism" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dirk Beyer · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kamakura_Budda_Daibutsu_front_1885.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Buddhism is based on teachings attributed to the Buddha, a wandering ascetic and religious teacher who lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a> in the 6th or 5th century BCE, the 5th century being the best estimate. The accounts of his life in the early texts are inconsistent. They give his family name as Gautama and place his birth at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lumbini">Lumbini</a>, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>. Legends make his father a king named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suddhodana">Suddhodana</a>, but scholars such as Richard Gombrich doubt this, since the evidence suggests he came from the Shakya community, governed by a small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oligarchy">oligarchy</a> or council.</p><p>The roots of the religion lie in the intellectual ferment of Iron Age India around the middle of the first millennium BCE, the period of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upanishads">Upanishads</a> and the rise of the Śramaṇa movements, which included Buddhism and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>According to tradition, the Buddha taught a path that leads to release from dukkha, suffering or unsatisfactoriness, by reaching nirvana, the extinguishing of the passions. He regarded it as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-way">Middle Way</a> between extreme asceticism and sensory indulgence. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/four-noble-truths">Four Noble Truths</a> hold that everything is impermanent, yet people crave and cling to impermanent things, which is dukkha, and that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/noble-eightfold-path">Noble Eightfold Path</a> leads out of the cycle of rebirth. Taking the Three Refuges, in the Buddha, the Dhamma and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sangha">Sangha</a>, is the traditional first step in most schools.</p><p>The Buddha's words were first passed on orally, and the first canonical texts were probably written down in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> about 400 years after his death. Scholars generally recognise two surviving branches, Theravāda and Mahāyāna. Theravada holds the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pali-canon">Pali Canon</a> as its only authority, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana</a>, with roots in the 1st century BCE, also honours later literature and stresses the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodhisattva">Bodhisattva</a> ideal of working for the liberation of all beings. Vajrayāna, which includes esoteric tantric techniques, may be seen as a separate branch or part of Mahayana.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Buddhism spread gradually through much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> and reached the West in the 19th century. Until the early second millennium it was widely practised in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a> before declining there. Learning centres such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nalanda">Nalanda</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikramashila">Vikramashila</a> preserved classical Indian disciplines, including grammar, astronomy and medicine, and taught foreign students from across Asia. The religion influenced the cultures of Asia deeply, in philosophy, art, architecture and festivals, and shaped the development of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taoism">Taoism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucianism">Confucianism</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Buddhism” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>For four centuries Ayutthaya was one of the principal powers of mainland Southeast Asia, and its institutions, court culture and <b>Buddhism</b> shaped the region well beyond its borders.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya Kingdom · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Buddhism</b>, brought from the Eastern Jin by the monk Marananta in 384, became the faith of the court, and the Sabi period saw great temples such as Jeongnimsa, whose five-storey stone pagoda still stands, and Mireuksa at Iksan, one of the largest temple complexes of its age, whose…</blockquote><a href="/empire/baekje">Baekje · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The elite wrote literary Chinese and practised <b>Buddhism</b>, building temples, stone lanterns and pagodas, while underfloor heating and roof-tile designs found at Balhae sites show continuities with Goguryeo.</blockquote><a href="/empire/balhae">Balhae · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Royal religion was Hindu, the brick sanctuaries of Isanapura centred on lingas, with Harihara, Brahma, Indra and Lakshmi also worshipped and <b>Buddhism</b> tolerated beside them.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chenla">Chenla · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Kaveripoompattinam near the delta and Nagappattinam were the major ports, which Ptolemy knew; they drew traders and several faiths, including <b>Buddhism</b>, and Roman coins of the early centuries CE have been found near the delta.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chola-empire">Chola Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The khanate's patronage of <b>Buddhism</b> and Zaya Pandita's Clear Script shaped Oirat and Kalmyk culture, and the script remained in use among Kalmyks and Oirats into the twentieth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dzungar-khanate">Dzungar Khanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
